Week 2 – Is AI the Right Tool?
My Beat: Real Estate
Artificial intelligence is becoming increasingly present in real estate. It can analyse property data, estimate prices, recommend properties to buyers, generate descriptions and even help investors identify opportunities. Because of this, it is tempting to think that almost every problem in real estate could be improved with AI. However, this week’s “No AI” framework made me look at the opposite question: when is AI actually not the right tool?
One problem where I believe AI is not the answer is the final decision of whether someone should buy or rent a particular home.
At first, this seems like a perfect problem for AI. A system could analyse the buyer’s budget, income, preferred location, property size, energy efficiency, historical prices and expected market development. It could then compare hundreds of properties and recommend the one that appears to be the best match. AI can definitely support this process, but I would not want it to make the final decision.
Why not AI?
The first reason is that choosing a home is not only a data problem. A property can look perfect according to measurable criteria and still feel completely wrong when you visit it. Maybe the street is noisier than expected, the apartment feels dark, the neighbourhood does not feel comfortable, or you simply cannot imagine yourself living there. On the other hand, someone might immediately connect with a property that does not receive the highest score from an algorithm.
Secondly, buying a home is a high-impact financial decision. For most people, it is one of the largest purchases they will ever make. An AI recommendation could be based on incomplete information or predictions that turn out to be wrong. For example, AI can analyse previous property prices, but it cannot know with certainty how interest rates, the neighbourhood or the housing market will develop.
There is also the question of personal priorities. Two people with exactly the same budget may make completely different choices. One person might prefer a small apartment in the centre of Ghent because they value being able to walk everywhere. Another might prefer a larger house outside the city because they value space and quietness. There is no objectively “correct” answer for AI to discover.
What should we use instead?
I think the better solution is AI-assisted human decision-making rather than AI decision-making. AI could filter listings, calculate affordability, compare prices per square metre, identify unusual prices and summarize relevant property information. This could save buyers a huge amount of time.
After that, however, the buyer should visit the property, research the neighbourhood, ask questions and potentially consult professionals such as a real-estate agent, bank or property expert before deciding.
Conclusion
For my real-estate beat, the “No AI” framework showed me an important distinction: just because AI can help with a problem does not mean it should make the decision. In real estate, AI is especially valuable when there is a large amount of information to process. But when a decision involves personal preferences, uncertainty and major financial consequences, AI should remain a supporting tool. The final choice of where to live should stay human.
